+/*
+ * Let the coprocessor reach a queue it has just been handed.
+ *
+ * SART is the only thing between ANS and memory on T8020: /arm-io/ans names
+ * sart-ans as its iommu-parent and there is no dart-ans anywhere in the ADT.
+ * Nothing permits the queues today. apple_nvme_sart_dma_setup() runs for RTKit
+ * shmem only, which is exactly why the firmware's syslog arrives and its first
+ * fetch of the admin submission queue does not:
+ *
+ *   assert failed: [7442]:cmd fetch error for host 0 Admin SQ, rresp code 2
+ *
+ * rresp 2 is a bus error on a DMA read, which is what a denied region looks
+ * like from the far side.
+ *
+ * SART wants both ends on a 4K boundary and a queue is usually smaller than
+ * that. Rounding to 16K instead, to match the granule every bootloader entry
+ * uses, was tried and is worse: the firmware then fails before it logs
+ * anything at all.
+ *
+ * Permitting the queues one at a time is not enough either. Identify completes
+ * that way, but the next admin command, a SMART log page, dies with
+ *
+ *   assert failed: [11956]:NVME_PERM_ERR ... mmu[fffffff7, ...]
+ *
+ * on a buffer the host allocated a moment earlier. The firmware writes that
+ * log out of its own DMA engine rather than through the NVMMU translation
+ * block the queues use, so every buffer the host hands it would have to be
+ * permitted, and there are sixteen SART entries in total. One entry across
+ * all of DRAM costs one of those sixteen and covers every case.
+ *
+ * ponytail: this hands ANS the whole of memory. It is the right trade for a
